WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:02.290
- Hey, Alex, I heard you
have a question for me.

00:00:02.290 --> 00:00:04.530
So, on his channel
- Yeah?

00:00:04.530 --> 00:00:07.680
- He's applying science to ordinary foods

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:09.690
in ways that he's trying to improve it.

00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:10.523
- Hey, Neil, what's up?

00:00:10.523 --> 00:00:11.397
My name is Alex, I'm a YouTuber

00:00:11.397 --> 00:00:13.390
and I make videos about food,

00:00:13.390 --> 00:00:16.590
but from the DIY and
science point of view.

00:00:16.590 --> 00:00:20.880
So my last video was about pan seasoning.

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:24.220
You know, the most common
and historical method

00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:27.437
to season a pan is to burn fat inside it,

00:00:27.437 --> 00:00:31.700
you create a dry, hard and
kind of non-stick surface.

00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:36.700
So, allegedly, oil polymerization
is responsible for that.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:40.480
Now, I am wondering, why does

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:43.710
that coating stick

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:46.730
to the material, the metal, in my case,

00:00:46.730 --> 00:00:49.330
carbon steel or even cast iron.

00:00:49.330 --> 00:00:53.560
And I'm also wondering, how could we make

00:00:53.560 --> 00:00:55.650
that bond stronger?

00:00:55.650 --> 00:00:58.790
Also, I know my question
is chemistry related

00:00:58.790 --> 00:01:00.770
and that you are an astrophysicist,

00:01:00.770 --> 00:01:03.710
so just imagine that
you're seasoning in space.

00:01:03.710 --> 00:01:04.671
Thank you.

00:01:04.671 --> 00:01:06.790
(laughter)

00:01:06.790 --> 00:01:09.130
- That's pretty funny, seasoning in space.

00:01:09.130 --> 00:01:10.995
- Just the way he speaks,
I want him to be my chef.

00:01:10.995 --> 00:01:12.130
- Without a doubt.

00:01:12.130 --> 00:01:13.856
- The food will be better
no matter what he's making.

00:01:13.856 --> 00:01:14.689
- Exactly.

00:01:14.689 --> 00:01:16.380
- He's making corn flakes, it's
gonna be French corn flakes.

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:18.020
- Absolutely, he can't cook
at all, it's just like,

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:19.477
he just tells you it's
good and you're like,

00:01:19.477 --> 00:01:20.947
"This is delicious!"

00:01:20.947 --> 00:01:22.287
"By ze way, you like dis."

00:01:22.287 --> 00:01:23.790
"Mmm, oh my god."

00:01:23.790 --> 00:01:25.510
- The accent makes it all better.

00:01:25.510 --> 00:01:28.354
- Exactly, "I have seasoned
this with my words."

00:01:28.354 --> 00:01:30.604
(laughter)

00:01:31.850 --> 00:01:35.340
- Sorry, that's the American
mentality manifested there.

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:39.000
So, first of all, the oil that makes

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:43.250
the surface of the pan
non-stick, which, by the way,

00:01:43.250 --> 00:01:47.180
is the secret of well-seasoned woks, okay?

00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:49.550
A good iron wok has oil

00:01:49.550 --> 00:01:51.140
that has been on there multiple times.

00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:54.250
So, the oil is now the surface of the pan

00:01:54.250 --> 00:01:56.960
and it's smooth, because the pan itself,

00:01:56.960 --> 00:01:59.250
the metallic surface is structured, okay.

00:01:59.250 --> 00:02:01.210
So, you put in oil and oil will fill all

00:02:01.210 --> 00:02:03.660
the nooks and crannies
and make a smooth surface

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:06.010
and then you burn it there, okay.

00:02:06.010 --> 00:02:09.730
So, then it just solidifies
that part that got burned.

00:02:09.730 --> 00:02:11.783
Polymerization is what he's talking about.

00:02:11.783 --> 00:02:14.853
My point is, beyond that,

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:18.300
it's stuck because it's
burned onto the pan.

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:20.860
You can remove it with a Brillo pad

00:02:20.860 --> 00:02:23.540
or with heavy soap, you can remove it.

00:02:23.540 --> 00:02:27.040
It's not permanently
affixed the way Teflon

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:30.040
is sort of permanently
attached, basically.

00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:31.660
You know, you can take a
belt sander and remove it,

00:02:31.660 --> 00:02:33.670
but otherwise, the Teflon is on.

00:02:33.670 --> 00:02:36.070
So, it's not permanently attached.

00:02:36.070 --> 00:02:38.500
It's there and you have to be careful

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:40.909
to not remove it when
it's time to wash it.

00:02:40.909 --> 00:02:43.490
So, no, it's not permanently attached.

00:02:43.490 --> 00:02:46.570
Why it's non-stick,
that's a really, really

00:02:46.570 --> 00:02:48.410
smooth surface at that point

00:02:48.410 --> 00:02:51.340
and not all smooth surfaces are non-stick,

00:02:51.340 --> 00:02:53.350
so I don't have a good answer for that.

00:02:53.350 --> 00:02:56.730
- So, I'll plead ignorance on this,

00:02:56.730 --> 00:02:59.270
but what I did see
recently is the frontier

00:02:59.270 --> 00:03:00.980
of non-stick surfaces, they're putting it

00:03:00.980 --> 00:03:03.340
on the inside of a ketchup bottle.

00:03:03.340 --> 00:03:04.490
- That makes sense.

00:03:04.490 --> 00:03:05.780
- So you tip the - and it just slides out.

00:03:05.780 --> 00:03:06.950
- It just slides out.

00:03:06.950 --> 00:03:09.110
- You tip that mayonnaise bottle

00:03:09.110 --> 00:03:10.130
the mayonnaise just slides out.

00:03:10.130 --> 00:03:11.210
- Okay, that sounds nasty.

00:03:11.210 --> 00:03:12.455
- That sounds wrong.

00:03:12.455 --> 00:03:13.994
(laughter)

00:03:13.994 --> 00:03:15.119
- You don't want mayonnaise coming to you.

00:03:15.119 --> 00:03:17.479
- I don't want mayonnaise sliding my way.

00:03:17.479 --> 00:03:19.210
No, I don't want any
mayonnaise sliding my way.

00:03:19.210 --> 00:03:20.230
- You wanna go in and get the mayonnaise.

00:03:20.230 --> 00:03:21.264
- Yes, exactly.

00:03:21.264 --> 00:03:22.207
- You don't want the mayonnaise
chasing you down the street.

00:03:22.207 --> 00:03:25.651
- I wanna manually mine my own mayonnaise.

00:03:25.651 --> 00:03:28.730
(laughter)

00:03:28.730 --> 00:03:31.500
- So the frontier of non-stick surfaces

00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:35.070
when introduced to food means
there's much less food waste,

00:03:35.070 --> 00:03:37.500
when trying to get the last of
the peanut butter in the jar,

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:40.470
the last of the mayonnaise,
the last of everything else.

00:03:40.470 --> 00:03:42.563
At home, we have a whole set of tools,

00:03:44.210 --> 00:03:45.043
what do you call them?

00:03:45.043 --> 00:03:45.876
- Little spatulas.

00:03:45.876 --> 00:03:46.709
- Spatulas, the little rubber -

00:03:46.709 --> 00:03:47.542
- Yeah, I use them, too.

00:03:47.542 --> 00:03:48.375
I get every little drop.

00:03:48.375 --> 00:03:49.208
- They're basically squeegees.

00:03:49.208 --> 00:03:50.290
- That's what they are.

00:03:50.290 --> 00:03:51.910
Yep, get it all.

00:03:51.910 --> 00:03:53.250
And I cut it open, too.

00:03:53.250 --> 00:03:54.450
- You know nature's first squeegee?

00:03:54.450 --> 00:03:55.283
- What?

00:03:55.283 --> 00:03:56.116
- The tongue.

00:03:56.116 --> 00:03:56.949
- There you go.

00:03:58.400 --> 00:03:59.233
- Right?

00:04:00.474 --> 00:04:02.640
That's what I do, I try not to do it

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:04.460
at a restaurant, but at
home, I'm licking the plate.

00:04:04.460 --> 00:04:06.000
If the sauce was good,
I'm licking the plate.

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:08.160
- My Sister told me that that's in France

00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:11.970
supposedly the highest
compliment you can pay a chef.

00:04:11.970 --> 00:04:13.570
Is if you take the plate and you, I mean

00:04:13.570 --> 00:04:14.730
you don't really ah-ah-ah.

00:04:14.730 --> 00:04:15.750
But it's basically
- Tactfully.

00:04:15.750 --> 00:04:17.650
- It's tactfully, one time and just like

00:04:17.650 --> 00:04:19.450
that's compliments to the chef.

00:04:19.450 --> 00:04:20.826
That's how good it was.

00:04:20.826 --> 00:04:21.659
- That's like in some other parts,

00:04:21.659 --> 00:04:22.733
if you burp, that's a good thing.

00:04:22.733 --> 00:04:26.207
- Exactly right, and that's
my Uncle Jim's house.

00:04:26.207 --> 00:04:27.610
(laughter)

00:04:27.610 --> 00:04:30.943
If you burp, it's compliments to the chef.

00:04:32.230 --> 00:04:33.063
- You don't have an Uncle Jim.

00:04:33.063 --> 00:04:34.560
- And by the way, this was great beer.

00:04:37.130 --> 00:04:39.160
- So, sorry, I only got
to half your answer there,

00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:40.400
but that's the best that I can do

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:43.950
as an astrophysicist, not,
it's not so much chemist,

00:04:43.950 --> 00:04:45.490
but material scientist.

00:04:45.490 --> 00:04:47.790
They're the ones who
think about the surfaces

00:04:47.790 --> 00:04:51.160
of solid objects and how they interact.

00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:52.610
So, you know what he does?

00:04:52.610 --> 00:04:56.180
He brings methods and
tools of science to food.

00:04:56.180 --> 00:04:57.700
Now, he's not the first to do that,

00:04:57.700 --> 00:04:59.870
but he does it in a very
fun and interesting way.

00:04:59.870 --> 00:05:02.700
You see all his failures and you look

00:05:02.700 --> 00:05:04.760
